<!--
  /studio/_layout.sui — the studio chrome wrapper. Every route under
  /studio/* is wrapped in <sui-studio-chrome>, which provides the
  studio rail + main content area + footer.

  The <slot name="route" /> is where the route content mounts. The
  host's `mountRoute(root, url, layouts, payload)` walks LAYOUTS,
  builds the chrome chain, and mounts the route content into the
  innermost <div slot="route">.

  Hub note: this file is a verbatim port from the shell. The shell's
  <sui-shell-bottom> is a shell-specific footer (account, status, etc.)
  the hub does not ship; per the port-studio contract, the page markup
  is byte-identical, and the missing <sui-shell-bottom> renders as an
  unregistered custom element (no-op at runtime, no compile error). The
  hub's root layout (`routes/_layout.sui` → `<ship-app-chrome>`)
  supplies the real top nav outside the studio chrome.

  Hub height-chain integration: the mount-router's `buildMountTarget`
  appends this chrome to the outer root chrome (a custom element with
  a named `<slot name="route">`) as a light-DOM child. The chrome's
  controller sets `slot="route"` on its host in `mount`, which triggers
  slot redistribution and projects the chrome into the route slot —
  without that, the chrome would be unslotted light-DOM content and
  invisible inside the host's display. See
  `apps/hub/src/components/sui-studio-chrome/sui-studio-chrome.controller.ts`.
-->

<sui-studio-chrome>
  <sui-studio-rail></sui-studio-rail>
  <main class="studio-main">
    <slot name="route" />
  </main>
  <sui-shell-bottom></sui-shell-bottom>
</sui-studio-chrome>

<style>
  sui-studio-chrome {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    grid-template-rows: 1fr auto;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: 100vh;
    background: var(--surface-0);
    color: var(--foreground);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
  }
  .studio-main {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1;
    overflow: auto;
  }
</style>
